  "live_records": {
    "q-89848": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89848",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216358/assets/496cfd2dc0864e509a1f3f958f2578cbe70684a19b93aecf89d6b38b8c7f5680.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph III has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
